00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:49.960
the birds.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:57.520
Vultures are a marvelous example of hyper efficiency in exploiting thermal currents in the air.

00:10:57.520 --> 00:10:59.080
And action David.

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:06.080
Those vultures roost and nest on ledges up there. And they're not early risers. That's

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:16.520
because they rely on the sun to get airborne.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:21.840
But to get even closer to the vultures, David and the team head up onto the gorge to a feeding

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:28.760
site.

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:34.720
To keep this griffon vulture population thriving, conservation worker Jesus Hernando regularly

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:37.240
brings dead livestock for the birds to feed on.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:43.640
There are lots of pig farms around here. They have an agreement that they'll bring up awful

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:49.600
animals that have died. The vultures love it. Absolutely love it.

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:55.920
The crew sets up, doing its best to blend in with the surroundings.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:02.280
Us strangers will spook them and scare them off. So to get around that, we are hiding all

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:09.760
the key members of crew in hides.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:17.420
Even with these measures in place, the team is still at the vulture's mercy.

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:23.520
If they decide that we're doing something a bit weird and they don't want to come along,

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:35.720
we could have a very boring day and not see a vulture at all.

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:50.760
With the crew now safely inside the hide, Jesus drops off a carcass.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:59.640
Four hours later, some vultures begin to circle, but they still don't land.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:03.760
Like many animals, they are very suspicious of danger. And if they see things which they

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:08.520
don't recognise and which hasn't happened before, they will be very leery about going

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:10.760
down. They don't risk it.

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:16.440
There's something putting them off. It's not even been a close encounter.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:23.040
The crew is running out of time. Just when they're about to give up hope, Jesus has one

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:27.000
last suggestion.

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:31.400
The mirror box is so big, Susi thinks that it will be a real problem, that the birds

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:36.260
will spot it. He's suggesting we put something over the top.

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:43.320
Now that the dazzling reflection of the camera's mirror box is covered up, the team try again.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:49.280
The crew watches the skies.

00:13:49.280 --> 00:14:00.400
Oh, there's one coming quite low. Oh, there's another one. This is encouraging. Come on,

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:01.400
babies.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:02.400
Oh, yeah.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:03.400
Come on.

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:08.000
Then at last, a bird descends, and a host of others quickly follow.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:15.280
This is very, very exciting. Finally, the vultures have landed.

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:19.480
Once the vultures have finished eating, they take advantage of the thermal currents and

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:23.600
