could ever have done.

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:35.660
It records at such high resolution, at around a thousand frames per second, that it can

00:23:35.660 --> 00:23:41.740
literally film detail too fast for the human eye to see. This will allow the team to capture

00:23:41.740 --> 00:23:46.360
hummingbirds in flight, as they've never been seen before.

00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:51.880
Hummingbirds really change the aerodynamic system they rely on fundamentally, and are

00:23:51.880 --> 00:23:57.840
able in consequence by beating their wings at extreme speeds and also rotating them in

00:23:57.840 --> 00:24:02.320
a particular way, in a figure of eight way, so that they get support on both the downbeat

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:07.920
and the upbeat, they're able to hover. There are other birds that can hover, but none as

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:11.360
perfectly as hummingbirds.

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:16.320
Scientist and hummingbird expert Doug Altshuler is on hand to help draw the birds in towards

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:21.800
the camera. And he's using sugared water, just as attractive as the nectar they feed

00:24:21.800 --> 00:24:23.600
on from flowers.

00:24:23.600 --> 00:24:31.000
He has an experiment that he hopes will demonstrate a unique property of hummingbird flight. He

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:36.480
places the nectar substitute on a mechanical slider that he's designed. It travels left

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:40.680
and right, a motion similar to a flower blowing in the wind.

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:43.200
Oh that's great.

00:24:43.200 --> 00:25:01.480
Oh I love the positioning. That was lovely. Complete 180 in the air.

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:07.440
And as the experiment shows, here slowed down around 40 times, the birds are perfectly capable

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:12.160
of manoeuvring to follow the food.

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:16.720
This filming trip has captured so much more than just the feeding. These delightful birds

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:18.120
are always happy to show off.

00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:23.240
To see them in slow motion, all of the beautiful acrobatics and the biomechanics just look

00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:28.240
stunning and it's proved a really successful trip. It really has been a kind of treasure

00:25:28.240 --> 00:25:55.680
trove of hummingbirds.

00:25:55.680 --> 00:26:00.220
The team leaves the cloud forest of Ecuador behind and heads for the country that houses

00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:10.080
some of the world's most diverse and rarest flying creatures, Borneo.

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:15.600
This final location is the most ambitious and challenging of the entire series. It's

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:20.640
also one that is close to David's heart, having first visited Borneo to meet its wildlife

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:27.920
in the 1950s. Today David will be lifted high up into the forest canopy to find a harlequin

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:33.480
tree frog. It's an animal that will illustrate how vertebrates took the first steps towards

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:41.040
flight. But recent drought conditions have made them even scarcer to find than usual.

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:46.520
To see a frog up close, David and cameraman Paul Williams, along with all of their equipment,

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:53.120
must be winched over 100 feet into the treetops. Having finally found a frog, it's time for

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:56.440
David to be strapped in and hoisted up into the air.

00:26:56.440 --> 00:27:04.560
It feels like it wants to tip you out. So that's why we've got the harness on and we'll

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:05.560
put a back-up line on the harness.

00:27:05.560 --> 00:27:06.560
I knew there'd be a reason for the harness.

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:07.560
OK.

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:08.560
Good luck.

00:27:08.560 --> 00:27:21.480
Up on Oren. Just face the tree, bend yourself off. The suspense is killing me.

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:29.960
Is that a joke? A hanging joke?

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:34.840
They need to get David up there quickly. Cameraman Paul has already been up the tree for over

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:38.400
two hours and the frog is raring to go.

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:51.120
We only have one frog, so if it decides to go, we will never find it in here.

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:56.240
Before David has a chance to complete the piece, the frog makes a break for freedom,

00:27:56.240 --> 00:28:00.520
straight into his microphone.

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:06.160
The chances of getting it if it does decide to jump are minimal. So we've got pretty much

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:09.480
one shot at this.

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:14.720
Shall I put him back?

00:28:14.720 --> 00:28:20.000
Shall I put him back?

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:21.000
Yeah.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:22.000
He's gone.

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:23.000
He's gone.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:30.000
The harlequin flying frog literally leapt out of the tree before we could even start filming,

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:31.000
which was a bit of a problem.

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:34.560
Lovely. There. Hold it. Thank you. Stand by. All set.

00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:40.760
For the professional, David carries on without the frog. Happy with the shots they've achieved,

00:28:40.760 --> 00:28:43.240
David's work on this sequence is complete.

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:48.840
This just shows David's incredible zest for adventure at the age of 88. He's prepared

00:28:48.840 --> 00:28:54.440
to be pulled up a tree. He wrote it in the script. I wasn't sure whether he was serious

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:57.840
that he was actually going to do it, but no, in the tip of the whetstone of his spirit,

00:28:57.840 --> 00:29:00.320
he was absolutely certain he was going to do it.

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:04.880
Despite the hiccups, when everything is cut together, it makes for an extraordinary scene.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:16.800
A snake, perhaps? Well, the tree frog has a remarkable trick for defence.

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:26.960
He glides.

00:29:26.960 --> 00:29:32.920
Its feet, with membranes between the toes, have been turned into four parachutes. These

00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:48.240
allow it to slow its descent and pull off a relatively safe landing.

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:53.840
The jungles of Borneo are a mysterious place. Some species are so secretive they've seldom

00:29:53.840 --> 00:30:00.960
been caught on camera before. Today's subject is even more extraordinary, as it's now extinct,

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:12.560
one of the first known vertebrates capable of powered flight, a pterosaur known as dimorphodon.

00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:17.320
The computer-generated pterosaurs will be seen swooping over David's head high up in

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:23.100
the forest canopy. And to achieve these aerial shots, they'll use a state-of-the-art camera

00:30:23.100 --> 00:30:30.360
system specially developed for the series, an octocopter, a drone uniquely adapted to

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:32.400
film ultra-high-resolution 3D.

00:30:32.400 --> 00:30:49.520
We're calling it the octocopter. It's a platform which has four propellers on either side.

00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:54.840
They're individually controllable so that you can make the thing move into wherever

00:30:54.840 --> 00:30:59.440
you want. And it also has gimbals on it so that it keeps it steady.

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:04.400
We're doing the maiden flight, which will be a first if we can capture a proper crystal-clear

00:31:04.400 --> 00:31:09.640
4K 3D fly around an octocopter, which of course in an amazing environment like this in the

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:13.720
Rome forest with David Attenborough in shot is pretty special.

00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:18.760
The 3D octocopter requires two cameras, meaning the machine has to bear double the weight

00:31:18.760 --> 00:31:24.840
it was initially designed to carry. Weighing around 10 kilos, a whole new stabilizing system

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:32.440
has been created to carry this extra weight. This is a huge risk for the team. Its first

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:39.040
and only test flight was in London, pretty different from the humid climate of the rainforest.

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:44.080
What's more, they're having to use two cameras borrowed from the main unit.

00:31:44.080 --> 00:31:48.120
It is a little bit nerve-racking. It's not impossible that something catastrophic might

00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:52.600
happen. So we are hoping for the best.

00:31:52.600 --> 00:31:58.000
If it goes down over this remote forest, it won't just be expensive. It could be a major

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:03.760
problem for the whole Borneo shoot. The team is in Borneo preparing to film David with

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:10.280
a pioneering piece of equipment, an octocopter fitted with ultra-high-resolution 3D cameras.

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:16.640
But this new technology has its limitations. Heat from its motors restrict the octocopter

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:21.700
to only a limited number of shots a day, and battery life allows just two and a half minutes

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:27.520
of flight at a time. So the octocopter and David must be precisely coordinated to get

00:32:27.520 --> 00:32:34.480
what they need in this small window of opportunity.

00:32:34.480 --> 00:32:38.940
To ensure David ducks from the imaginary pterosaurs at the right moment, he'll have to listen

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:43.680
for instructions called to him by the director.

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:48.240
The octocopter carrying the two huge 3D cameras is about to go, and we're going to see what

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:51.240
happens.

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:59.640
We've got about two and a half minutes flying time. We've got to be very quick from this

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:01.760
moment in.

00:33:01.760 --> 00:33:04.760
It's up in the air. Everything is going according to plan.

00:33:04.760 --> 00:33:07.040
So far, so good.

00:33:07.040 --> 00:33:12.000
But within moments, they realise there's something they haven't considered. The noise of the

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:15.280
octocopter is far louder than they'd first thought.

00:33:15.280 --> 00:33:19.280
David, unfortunately, can't hear.

00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:22.280
I can hear!

00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:25.280
OK, I'm going over.

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:34.040
So they're now really on the edge of just pushing it to the absolute limit.

00:33:34.040 --> 00:33:39.160
Eventually, David and the camera team find a way to communicate over the noise. And just

00:33:39.160 --> 00:33:43.840
in time, the octocopter has run out of power.

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:51.040
When the CGI pterosaurs are added months later, the whole scene really comes to life.

00:33:51.040 --> 00:34:08.880
This is dimorphodon, and it had real power in the air.

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:14.520
There are only a few days left in Borneo, and the last location lies in a cave deep

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:19.480
in the heart of the jungle. But this journey will be worth it, for this is the kingdom

00:34:19.480 --> 00:34:26.800
of a remarkable kind of mammal, the only one capable of flight, the bat.

00:34:26.800 --> 00:34:33.000
Birds actually have left one particular niche largely unoccupied. That is, say, the night

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:37.360
sky. And there are actually a lot of things in the air at night, a lot of insects, a lot

00:34:37.360 --> 00:34:43.920
of food. But very few birds have evolved to take advantage of that. There was a niche

00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:53.400
that was ready for exploitation. And the early insect-eating animals, insectivores, mammals,

00:34:53.400 --> 00:34:57.200
evolved wings in order to pursue that.

00:34:57.200 --> 00:35:02.760
Here the spectacular vast caves of Gomantong have a ceiling that reaches to around 450

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:08.480
feet. There in the darkness dwells one of the largest colonies of bats in the world.

00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:13.080
And then you come and you see this vast entrance, and then your eyes begin to adjust, and you

00:35:13.080 --> 00:35:21.120
look up and you see these vast openings which light is pouring through. And you look up,

00:35:21.120 --> 00:35:28.720
and you literally see millions of bats.

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:33.240
To get close enough to film the bats, the team must head to the very back of the cave

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:50.040
where they roost. And with over a ton of equipment to carry, it's a back-breaking task.

00:35:50.040 --> 00:35:51.840
It's a truly bizarre place.

00:35:51.840 --> 00:36:01.120
The guano, the droppings from the bats, has accumulated an enormous dune which is 20,

00:36:01.120 --> 00:36:09.120
30 metres high. When you approach it, in certain parts of the day, it seems to have a golden

00:36:09.120 --> 00:36:16.840
twinkle over the surface, a color-skating gold. I mean, it's really, really very beautiful.

00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:23.480
But if you look closer at the alluring sparkle, it reveals something rather unpleasant.

00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:26.640
Everywhere you look, there's cockroaches crawling all over you, crawling through your hair,

00:36:26.640 --> 00:36:31.460
crawling all over your body.

00:36:31.460 --> 00:36:34.200
It's a less than ideal working environment.

00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:40.280
It's pretty close to hell on Earth, so it's obviously absolutely stinks.

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:44.880
Yeah, I don't think this is probably the most attractive place most of the crew have

00:36:44.880 --> 00:36:48.400
ever been. Somebody told me it's the biggest guano pile in the world. I don't know if

00:36:48.400 --> 00:36:50.480
that's true, but it certainly looks it.

00:36:50.480 --> 00:36:54.480
You're being rained on constantly by an bat poo, bat pee.

00:36:54.480 --> 00:36:58.240
Nice big one. Hello.

00:36:58.240 --> 00:37:01.440
Bat poo.

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:13.880
For David, this unearthly location has a very special significance. He first visited this

00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:21.960
cave to film over 40 years ago. So returning many years later is a poignant moment.

00:37:21.960 --> 00:37:28.640
It may well be since I am now over the last years. This may be the last major trip I do

00:37:28.640 --> 00:37:36.280
in the tropics, in which case Comanton, as it were, bookends my last section of my career

00:37:36.280 --> 00:37:39.840
as a program maker.

00:37:39.840 --> 00:37:44.520
To illustrate the sheer number of bats, David's piece to camera is delivered at the base of

00:37:44.520 --> 00:37:47.920
the guano pile.

00:37:47.920 --> 00:37:53.600
That comes from an army of cockroaches, which are chewing their way through the bat droppings

00:37:53.600 --> 00:37:58.120
to extract the last particles of nutriment.

00:37:58.120 --> 00:38:02.800
The first shots of the day are done, but the team's greatest challenge is yet to come.

00:38:02.800 --> 00:38:06.720
There's one sequence in this whole series which we've invested more in than anything

00:38:06.720 --> 00:38:12.960
else. It's where bats, millions of bats, fly out the top of this extraordinary cave here.

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:18.800
The bats come out altogether in a very short period, like 10 minutes, 15 minutes. So there's

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:25.240
this torrent, this blizzard of bats.

00:38:25.240 --> 00:38:31.680
To truly do this sequence justice, the crew has to be split into two units. One unit will

00:38:31.680 --> 00:38:35.480
film the bat exodus from the exit right at the top of the cave.

00:38:35.480 --> 00:38:40.920
We decided that it would be a jolly good idea if Paul, the cameraman, hung in the mouth

00:38:40.920 --> 00:38:45.960
of the cave as the bats flew past him.

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:51.960
The main unit will remain on the ground to prepare David for his piece to camera. Careful

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:56.800
coordination is vital.

00:38:56.800 --> 00:39:08.200
Paul Williams sets up their camera hundreds of feet at the top of the cave.

00:39:08.200 --> 00:39:12.400
The bats will all stream out behind me, so hopefully we'll try and get the camera out

00:39:12.400 --> 00:39:18.280
on this line and then lower me into the stream of bats. That's the plan.

00:39:18.280 --> 00:39:23.520
Meanwhile on the ground, David straps in. He will be lifted high into the air for his

00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:28.440
piece to camera, filmed by the octocopter fitted with a 3D camera.

00:39:28.440 --> 00:39:35.040
Dusk approaches. Both teams are set to go. But then, just as Paul is about to be suspended

00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:38.960
over the mouth of the cave, the team discovers a technical fault with the camera.

00:39:38.960 --> 00:39:48.960
We are in danger of missing the bats. They aren't going to move on so we'll just cross

00:39:48.960 --> 00:39:52.240
over to get the camera ready.

00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:58.960
Precious minutes tick by as the technical team work desperately to fix the problem.

00:39:58.960 --> 00:40:03.920
While this goes on, the unit on the ground winch David 200 feet up into the cave to the

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:12.160
point where millions of bats will swarm past him.

00:40:12.160 --> 00:40:25.400
The 3D octocopter is the only way to film David suspended in this spectacular scene.

00:40:25.400 --> 00:40:30.480
Without a moment to lose, the camera at the top of the cave is fixed and Paul is quickly

00:40:30.480 --> 00:40:43.440
lowered into the maelstrom of bats. They're just in time.

00:40:43.440 --> 00:40:49.480
Minutes later, David begins his piece to camera for the octocopter while above Paul is rolling

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:54.360
too. Millions of bats now pour from the cave.

00:40:54.360 --> 00:41:04.960
They were on all sides coming through me and I kept hearing the water barrel on the bat.

00:41:04.960 --> 00:41:09.240
I kept hearing them hitting that. Had one hit my head. I think we've got some fabulous

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:14.000
stuff there actually. I really do.

00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:19.200
With his piece to camera delivered, David is finally lowered back to the cave floor.

00:41:19.200 --> 00:41:24.640
This sequence may be one of the most astonishing of his entire career.

00:41:24.640 --> 00:41:32.720
It's really an extraordinary privileged view up there and it really is fantastic.

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:36.760
I'm forgetting what it was.

00:41:36.760 --> 00:41:41.800
Well done guys. It is a wrap. Well done.

00:41:41.800 --> 00:41:47.040
To sit there with one of the greatest natural history makers of all time and he sort of

00:41:47.040 --> 00:41:51.800
says to you, this footage is amazing, the teams do a great job. That's a pretty special

00:41:51.800 --> 00:41:52.800
feeling.

00:41:52.800 --> 00:41:57.760
When the shots of the two units are combined, the result is a sequence that demonstrates

00:41:57.760 --> 00:42:03.000
how this is a truly ground breaking series.

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:07.620
Outside lying in wait for the bats are hunters.

00:42:07.620 --> 00:42:12.920
Hunters belonging to that other great group of animals with which they share the skies.

00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:25.640
Birds.

00:42:25.640 --> 00:42:31.600
Certainly the most ambitious 3D project we've done. I didn't hold back and write the script.

00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:36.360
I wrote everything that I thought the story demanded. And it tells a serious scientific

00:42:36.360 --> 00:42:47.560
story and yet at the same time it will remain visually stunning.
